1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:02,160
This is every plane in the sky,

2
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:03,800
live, right now.

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,670
Every satellite overhead.

4
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:07,310
Every ship at sea.

5
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:09,290
And it all runs in your browser,

6
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:10,160
for free.

7
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:12,080
It's called God's Eye View,

8
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:13,850
and its tagline says it all:

9
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:15,860
a spy satellite simulator,

10
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:17,570
except the data is real.

11
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:19,970
It hit number one on GitHub Trending,

12
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:22,320
and it's past 50,000 stars.

13
00:00:22,690 --> 00:00:24,360
Here's what you're actually looking at.

14
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:27,760
Over 11,000 live aircraft from public

15
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:29,860
flight trackers, with military flights on

16
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:30,750
their own layer.

17
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:33,660
Ships stream in from AIS,

18
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:35,930
the radio beacons vessels broadcast at

19
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:36,500
sea.

20
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:40,140
838 satellites, each one moving along its

21
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:40,970
real orbit.

22
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:42,890
Earthquakes from the last day.

23
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:45,030
Fires spotted from space.

24
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:48,380
Rain radar, lightning, cyclones and wind.

25
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,480
And then it gets a little unsettling.

26
00:00:50,910 --> 00:00:53,340
Nearly 4,000 public traffic cameras,

27
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,860
from Texas to London to Finland,

28
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:57,550
pinned right onto the globe.

29
00:00:57,850 --> 00:00:59,110
Now flip the sensor.

30
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:01,040
Night vision. Thermal.

31
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:02,830
An old CRT screen.

32
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:04,980
And your browser looks like a surveillance

33
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,270
feed from a spy movie.

34
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:09,350
Zoom in, and cities rise in 3D,

35
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,490
built from Google's photorealistic map

36
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:12,030
tiles.

37
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:13,900
Add an OpenAI key,

38
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,450
and you can even steer it with your voice.

39
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,620
17 of its 20 layers need no API key at

40
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:21,600
all. Clone it, run it,

41
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:23,630
and the planet opens in your browser.

42
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:26,350
The catch: its own warning says the data

43
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:28,890
can be delayed, incomplete or wrong,

44
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,380
so it's not for navigation or emergencies.

45
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,120
Traffic is simulated, camera angles are

46
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:36,890
estimates, and the 3D cities need a key.

47
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:39,440
And by design, it won't do face

48
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:41,800
recognition or track individual people.

49
00:01:42,030 --> 00:01:44,100
All of this data was already public.

50
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:46,240
It's just never been this easy to see it

51
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:47,150
all at once.

52
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:49,370
That's God's Eye View.

53
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,010
Free, open source, and a little bit

54
00:01:52,030 --> 00:01:52,780
terrifying.

55
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:55,760
Subscribe, there's a new tool every day.
